(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-atherosclerosis-lipids-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 4","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 47-year-old woman with familial dysbetalipoproteinemia asks what the disorder means for her long-term health. Which complication is it associated with?","choices":["Accelerated atherosclerosis","Loss of vision","Recurrent acute pancreatitis","Sudden death in childhood"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia is associated with accelerated atherosclerosis, the long-term danger of its remnant-driven rise in triglyceride and cholesterol. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 56"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman with familial chylomicronemia syndrome has had two attacks of pancreatitis. Her fasting triglyceride is 1500 mg/dL. Which management plan is most appropriate?","choices":["Ezetimibe and a normal diet","Apheresis every two weeks","Very low-fat diet and a fibrate","High-intensity statin alone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Triglyceride above 500 mg/dL is managed with a very low-fat diet (under 15% of calories from fat), weight management, physical activity and a fibrate, niacin or both. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 118"},{"q":"A 26-year-old woman with a borderline triglyceride of 170 mg/dL is starting a combined birth control pill. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["She must avoid all dietary fat","She needs a fibrate before starting","Her lipids should be monitored","Estrogen lowers all her lipids"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Estrogen can elevate triglyceride (while increasing high-density lipoprotein), so patients starting birth control pills or postmenopausal estrogen, or who are pregnant, are monitored. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 41-year-old man has a high-density lipoprotein of 32 mg/dL. He smokes a pack a day and is 30 pounds overweight. Which single change can raise his high-density lipoprotein by up to 10%?","choices":["Moderate alcohol","Low-carbohydrate diet","Taking a fibrate","Smoking cessation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Smoking cessation increases high-density lipoprotein up to 10%; losing 6 pounds raises it about 1 mg/dL, and frequent aerobic exercise about 5%. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 112"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with a prior heart attack and symptomatic peripheral arterial disease takes a maximal statin, and his low-density lipoprotein is 82 mg/dL. Which change is inappropriate at this point?","choices":["Adding evolocumab before ezetimibe","Adding ezetimibe to the statin","Continuing the maximal statin","Rechecking lipids after the change"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. If the injectable class is considered, ezetimibe is added to the maximal statin before it; skipping that step is out of sequence and less cost-effective. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A 9-year-old boy has untreated homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia, and testing shows under 2% of normal low-density lipoprotein receptor activity. His parents ask about the outlook without treatment. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["Pancreatitis is the main threat","Survival past the second decade is rare","Heart disease is unlikely before 60","He will outgrow the disorder"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Untreated receptor-negative patients (under 2% of normal activity) rarely survive past the second decade, and accelerated atherosclerosis can cause sudden death in childhood. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man with untreated heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia asks why treatment matters when he feels well. Which complication is he at risk of?","choices":["Sudden death in childhood","Loss of vision","A premature heart attack","Acute pancreatitis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia accounts for up to 5% of premature heart attacks in men under 55 and women under 65, and it causes premature atherosclerotic disease. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man with a triglyceride of 250 mg/dL drinks several sugary sodas and eats white bread and pasta at every meal. Which dietary counseling point addresses a cause of his triglyceride?","choices":["Eat more sugar at night","Diet cannot change it","Cut excess carbohydrate","Add more saturated fat"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Excess carbohydrate increases hepatic very-low-density triglyceride secretion, raising triglyceride, so reducing it addresses a secondary cause. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 45-year-old man has an isolated high-density lipoprotein of 34 mg/dL with normal low-density lipoprotein and triglyceride. Before treatment is planned, which conditions should be excluded?","choices":["Pregnancy and kidney disease","Nephrotic syndrome and anorexia","Pancreatitis and gallstones","Diabetes, smoking and obesity"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. With low high-density lipoprotein, rule out diabetes mellitus, cigarette smoking and obesity, then address them as the main levers. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"A 51-year-old man with obesity and type 2 diabetes has a triglyceride of 720 mg/dL, elevated total cholesterol, reduced high-density lipoprotein and a normal low-density lipoprotein. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia","Severe hypertriglyceridemia","Mixed dyslipidemia","Heterozygous familial form"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Triglyceride above 500 with elevated total cholesterol and reduced high-density lipoprotein, usually without raised low-density lipoprotein, defines it, typically on a polygenic background with obesity or insulin resistance. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man has had both a heart attack and an ischemic stroke. On a maximal statin plus ezetimibe his low-density lipoprotein is 88 mg/dL. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Add evolocumab or alirocumab","Replace the statin with a fibrate","No change, as he is below 100","Add a bile acid sequestrant only"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In very high-risk disease, if low-density lipoprotein stays 70 or more on a maximally tolerated statin and ezetimibe, adding a proprotein convertase subtilisin/kexin type 9 inhibitor is reasonable. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 100"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man with a triglyceride of 380 mg/dL is already on a moderate-intensity statin and at his low-density goal. Which drug is the most effective choice to add for his triglyceride?","choices":["Alirocumab","Fenofibrate","Colestipol","Ezetimibe"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Fibrates such as fenofibrate are the most effective for lowering triglyceride, by 20% to 50%, and they also raise high-density lipoprotein 10% to 30%. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 86"},{"q":"A 34-year-old bodybuilder has a high-density lipoprotein of 22 mg/dL. He admits to using anabolic steroids for two years. Which counseling point is most appropriate?","choices":["He needs a statin to fix the low value","His diet alone explains the low value","The steroids are lowering his good cholesterol","Low values like his carry no heart risk"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Anabolic steroids decrease high-density lipoprotein, so they are a secondary cause of his low level that stopping them addresses. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A healthy 22-year-old man establishes care. He has never had cholesterol tested and has no symptoms or family history of heart disease. What is the most appropriate step regarding lipids?","choices":["No testing until he turns 40","Test only if xanthomas appear","Coronary calcium score first","Screen for high blood cholesterol"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Screening of all adults aged 20 years or older for high blood cholesterol is recommended, whatever their symptoms; most dyslipidemia is found on laboratory testing. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man whose brother had a heart attack at 50 has lipoprotein(a) measured at 85 mg/dL. His low-density lipoprotein is 140 mg/dL. What does the lipoprotein(a) result mean for his management?","choices":["It changes nothing in his plan","He needs aggressive low-density lowering","It is protective and lowers his risk","He needs a fibrate for the value"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Elevated lipoprotein(a) is a causal and independent risk factor for atherosclerotic disease, and a patient with it needs aggressive therapy to decrease low-density lipoprotein. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man without cardiovascular disease has hypertension and smokes. His clinician wants to estimate his 10-year risk of atherosclerotic disease to guide statin therapy. Which tool is appropriate?","choices":["Pooled Cohort Equation","Skin biopsy for receptors","Fundoscopic examination","Metabolic syndrome criteria"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. It estimates 10-year primary risk of atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease in patients 40 to 79 without pre-existing disease, using variables such as age, sex, cholesterol, systolic pressure, diabetes and smoking. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man had a heart attack last year. He stopped his high-intensity statin because of muscle aches, which then resolved. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Moderate-intensity statin","Stop all lipid therapy","Fish oil in place of statins","A fibrate instead"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. If a high-intensity statin is not tolerated in clinical atherosclerotic disease, a moderate-intensity statin is used, so he still receives secondary prevention. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 97"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman has just been diagnosed with heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia. She has three children aged 4, 7 and 12. What is the most appropriate step for her children?","choices":["Start each child on a statin now","Lipid screening for each child","Wait until each child turns 20","Skin biopsy for each child now"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Identifying a primary cause influences the management of other family members, and elective screening is appropriate as young as 2 years with a family history of high cholesterol. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 94"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes has low-density lipoprotein of 130 mg/dL. She has no atherosclerotic disease and no other risk factors. What is the most appropriate lipid therapy?","choices":["Fibrate monotherapy","Moderate-intensity statin","Ezetimibe as monotherapy","Lifestyle changes alone"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Adults 40 to 75 with diabetes and low-density lipoprotein of 70 or more start a moderate-intensity statin without calculating 10-year risk. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 102"},{"q":"A 10-year-old girl is seen for a well-child visit. She is healthy, and there is no known family history of lipid disorders. Which lipid test is appropriate?","choices":["No test until age 20","Skin biopsy for receptors","A non-fasting lipid profile","A fasting panel after 12 hours"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pediatric patients are screened with a non-fasting lipid profile once between ages 9 and 11, and again later in adolescence. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"A 30-year-old woman with obesity and insulin resistance has a fasting triglyceride of 1400 mg/dL, reduced high-density lipoprotein and a normal low-density lipoprotein. She feels well. Which complication is she at greatest immediate risk of?","choices":["Achilles tendon rupture","Sudden death in childhood","Hypothyroid crisis","Acute pancreatitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Triglyceride above 500 mg/dL carries a risk of acute pancreatitis, and hers is well past that threshold even though she feels well. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 43"},{"q":"A 36-year-old woman has just been told she has heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia. She asks whether any single test can prove it. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["Fundoscopy settles the question","No definitive test exists for her form","A skin biopsy proves it in her case","A calcium score of zero rules it out"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. No definitive diagnostic test is available for the heterozygous form; the skin biopsy with receptor activity confirms only the homozygous disorder. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman with dyslipidemia weighs 200 pounds and asks how much weight she should aim to lose. Which goal is most appropriate?","choices":["About 1 to 2 pounds","About 60 to 80 pounds","No weight loss is needed","About 10 to 20 pounds"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Therapeutic lifestyle changes aim for a 5% to 10% reduction of initial weight; for 200 pounds that is 10 to 20 pounds. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man has low-density lipoprotein of 185 mg/dL and a triglyceride of 320 mg/dL. He has had no pancreatitis. Which initial approach is inappropriate?","choices":["Weight loss and more activity","Reviewing his alcohol history","A fibrate first, deferring the statin","A statin to lower the cholesterol"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Low-density lipoprotein is the primary target and is addressed first; triglyceride therapy is added only if triglyceride stays above 200 once the low-density goal is met. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 116"},{"q":"A 32-year-old man has a white-gray ring around the edge of each cornea (corneal arcus). His low-density lipoprotein is 250 mg/dL with a normal triglyceride, and his father died of a heart attack at 52. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia","Heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","Homozygous familial hypercholesterolemia","Familial chylomicronemia syndrome"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Corneal arcus before 40 usually indicates a genetic condition, and low-density lipoprotein of 200 to 400 with normal triglyceride and premature family coronary disease fits the heterozygous form, in which arcus is common. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 36"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man without diabetes has low-density lipoprotein of 160 mg/dL and a 10-year atherosclerotic risk of 24%. After discussion he agrees to treatment. What should therapy aim for?","choices":["Lower low-density lipoprotein 10% to 20%","Lower triglyceride below 150 first","Raise high-density lipoprotein above 60","Lower low-density lipoprotein 50% or more"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When 10-year risk is high (20% or more), the statin should reduce low-density lipoprotein by 50% or more; at intermediate risk the target is 30% or more. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 104"},{"q":"A 47-year-old man started a moderate-intensity statin eight weeks ago. His low-density lipoprotein has fallen from 160 to 104 mg/dL. How is his response to therapy judged?","choices":["By the percentage fall from baseline","By whether total cholesterol is 200","By whether any xanthomas persist","By his high-density lipoprotein alone"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Responses to lifestyle and statin therapy are defined by percentage reductions in low-density lipoprotein from baseline; his 35% fall matches a moderate-intensity response of 30% to under 50%. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 107"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman has a waist circumference of 38 inches, triglyceride of 190 mg/dL, high-density lipoprotein of 45 mg/dL, blood pressure of 134/88 and fasting glucose of 104 mg/dL. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Metabolic syndrome","Mixed dyslipidemia","Severe hypertriglyceridemia","Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Any three of five criteria establish it; she meets four: waist above 35 inches, triglyceride above 150, high-density lipoprotein below 50 in a woman and blood pressure above 130/85. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 38"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man has low-density lipoprotein of 178 mg/dL. He reports fatigue, weight gain and cold intolerance over the past year. Before a statin is started, which test should be ordered?","choices":["Thyroid function tests","Coronary artery calcium score","Fasting triglyceride only","Skin biopsy for receptor activity"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Decreased thyroid hormone down-regulates hepatic low-density lipoprotein receptors and raises low-density lipoprotein, so elevated levels call for screening for low thyroid before a statin. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman with heterozygous familial hypercholesterolemia still has low-density lipoprotein of 210 mg/dL despite a high-intensity statin, ezetimibe and nicotinic acid taken reliably. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Stop the statin and add a fibrate","Refer for liver transplantation","Recheck in a year and change nothing","Add evolocumab or alirocumab"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Patients refractory to drug therapy may be treated with a proprotein convertase subtilisin/kexin type 9 inhibitor such as evolocumab or alirocumab, an injectable monoclonal antibody approved for familial hypercholesterolemia. — 22. Atherosclerosis and Lipid Disorders 2026 Carter.pptx, Slide 110"}]};
